course: dictionary
lesson: 5374
title: A word from old stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old, and you mostly meet them in fairy tales, nursery rhymes and old books. Knave is one of them. Read the words around it to find its meaning. «The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts, All on a summer day: The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts, And took them quite away!» Someone who steals tarts is dishonest, so a knave is a dishonest person.
explain: Knave has more than one meaning. Old books use it for a dishonest man who tricks people, a rogue. Some old books also use it for a boy or young male servant. In a card game, knave is an old name for the jack, the card with a young man's picture, between the ten and the queen.
explain: Context helps you choose the right meaning. In a rhyme about tarts, knave means a rogue, not a card. In a card game, it means the jack. Always check your meaning by putting it into the sentence and asking if it still makes sense.
explain: In Alice in Wonderland, the Knave of Hearts carries the King's crown on a crimson velvet cushion in a grand procession, and he is put on trial for stealing the Queen's tarts. Stories use the word knave to tell the reader that a character is not to be trusted.
fact: Knave is an old word, found in fairy tales, nursery rhymes and old books.
fact: «Knave» can mean a dishonest man who tricks people, a rogue.
fact: In playing cards, knave is an old name for the jack.
fact: «The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts» is from a real old nursery rhyme.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, the Knave of Hearts is put on trial for stealing the Queen's tarts.
exercise: In the rhyme, what did the Knave of Hearts do? | Baked the tarts | *Stole the tarts | Sang a happy song | Gave the tarts away to the King
exercise: In a card game, what is a knave? | A king with a crown | A card that is always worth ten | *The jack, the card with a young man's picture | The ace of spades
exercise: In the rhyme, which word tells you the Knave took the tarts? | summer | day | Queen | *stole
test: Which meaning fits best? «The knave tricked everyone who trusted him.» | *A dishonest man who tricks people | A kind helper who cooks for the family | A golden crown | A card with a queen on it
test: Why does the word knave tell a reader not to trust a character? | Because it always means a young prince | Because it is a long word | *Because a knave is someone who is dishonest | Because knaves are always kind
exam: What does knave mean in the sentence «The Knave of Hearts, he stole the tarts»? | *A dishonest person | A card game played with hearts | A king who wears a crown | A servant who bakes the tarts
